Z||000021||Black88GT||07-17-2001||09:47 PM||||Actually I did some research and GM was not BORN in Canada.  What happened was these  McLaughlin brothers (Sam and George) were canadians interested in building cars.  So they rode in a Buick, belonging to their company's bookkeeper.  They both liked it a lot but they weren't convinced, so they did more research.  By 1905 they rode most cars availible at the time amd decided to build the Buick in Oshawa.  At frst, they had problems making a deal with William Durant (born in Boston, raised in Flint) who was a partner in the Buick company, but then one of their engineers got sick and the plans for their own company fell through, so they called Durant in Michigan.  Durant sent 2 Buick engineers over and they began production of the "McLaughlins" in 1908, with Buick engines, in Oshawa.  <P>At first they were a hit (the McLaughlins), but then sales started to decline.  They pushed the execs to change their name to Buick, but they settled on McLaughlin-Buick.<P>In 1908, Durant, an <B>AMERICAN</B>,  formed General Motors from Oldsmobile and Buick.  GM of Canada wasn't formed until 1918 when George and Sam wanted to get out of the business, however part of the deal was they had to stay to run it.  They were the first president/vice-president of GM of Canada.<P>So there you have it, the real history of GM.  Most resources were <A HREF="http://www.gm.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gm.com</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.gmcanada.com." TARGET=_blank>www.gmcanada.com.</A>   <P><BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||EdsB52||07-18-2001||01:16 AM||||Sounds like an airtight argument to me. I agree with your assessment of American vs. German cars. If superior engineering costs 5 times more, is it really more efficient or 'better?' Since none of us in here are likely to be millionaires, cost is a huge factor. I think some Japanese stuff is the most reliable when it comes to banging the crap out of it and neglecting maintenance, German stuff is probably the most precise, and American stuff is the most cost effective (miles per dollar). <P>Here's another research subject that I find interesting. Canada originally used the English measurement system, right? When did Canada convert to the metric system?||||reg||1
Z||000023||firstfiero||07-18-2001||10:51 AM||||      Next time your at a book store black88gt pick up a consumer reports car buying hand book. Just look through the gm section. You'll notice the pages full of black circles...the worst rating that you can get. So when I make the statement I'm NOT basing it on my experience with the one car. As for vw I agree they haven't been makeing the best cars in the last years either. My father in law just got a golf turbo diesel and it just about got him killed twice. When he pulled out into traffic it just stalled and he almost got killed. A rabbit diesel will last forever! Diesels are just made for high mileage. <BR>    My challenge is this...find one place with technical data not showing american cars are junk. I can't find one!<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||EdsB52||07-18-2001||11:23 AM||||I won't totally disagree with you firstfiero, but what are the costs like for each vehicle? If you spend twice as much for the cummulative price and maintenance of an imported car over the price of an American one, is it really a better deal? I think the vast majority of members in here love to thrash and then rebuild their cars. Would that be possible with all foreign cars, especially German made ones? How about Italian made cars? I've had my 83 Chevy pu for 12 years, my girlfriend has been around for 10; seniority has its privledges!<P>Remember, data is political and data can be skewed. If you're an editor and you want a car that will never need maintenance, but don't car if it's ugly and slow, you might consider different attributes than we would here in the club. So I guess the moral to the story is; buy your wife an imported mini-van, and buy yourself a Fiero, Camaro, or Corvette. Fair solution?||||reg||1
